### Logic Description Language

This revision responds to the feedback on the first report. The grammar now
distinguishes input and output port syntax where this can be done cleanly, and
it requires at least one declared device. Some checks must still remain
semantic, because whether a particular port is valid depends on the declared
device type and, for gates, the declared number of inputs.


### Revised EBNF

network =
    devices_section , connections_section , monitors_section , "END" , ";" ;

devices_section =
    "DEVICES" , "{" , device_decl , { device_decl } , "}" ;

device_decl =
    device_name , ":" , device_spec , ";" ;

device_spec =
      "SWITCH" , "(" , bit , ")"
    | "CLOCK" , "(" , positive_integer , ")"
    | gate_kind , "(" , positive_integer , ")"
    | "DTYPE"
    | "XOR" ;

gate_kind =
      "AND"
    | "NAND"
    | "OR"
    | "NOR" ;

connections_section =
    "CONNECT" , "{" , { connection_decl } , "}" ;

connection_decl =
    output_signal , "->" , input_signal , ";" ;

monitors_section =
    "MONITOR" , "{" , [ output_signal , { "," , output_signal } ] , "}" , ";" ;

input_signal =
    device_name , "." , input_port ;

output_signal =
      device_name
    | device_name , "." , dtype_output_port ;

input_port =
      gate_input_port
    | dtype_input_port ;

gate_input_port =
      "I1" | "I2" | "I3" | "I4" | "I5" | "I6" | "I7" | "I8"
    | "I9" | "I10" | "I11" | "I12" | "I13" | "I14" | "I15" | "I16" ;

dtype_input_port =
    "DATA" | "CLK" | "SET" | "CLEAR" ;

dtype_output_port =
    "Q" | "QBAR" ;

device_name =
    letter , { letter | digit | "_" } ;

positive_integer =
    non_zero_digit , { digit } ;

bit =
    "0" | "1" ;

letter =
    "A" | ... | "Z" | "a" | ... | "z" ;

digit =
    "0" | ... | "9" ;

non_zero_digit =
    "1" | ... | "9" ;


### Notes on the Revision

The original grammar used identical input_name and output_name rules. That was
too permissive, because it allowed many obviously wrong signals to parse and
then forced the implementation to catch them later as semantic errors. The
revised grammar separates input_port from dtype_output_port. Therefore examples
such as G1.OUT or G1.HELLO are syntax errors rather than semantic errors.

The grammar still cannot completely replace semantic checking. For example,
G1.I16 is syntactically an input signal, but if G1 is declared as NAND(2), then
G1.I16 is semantically invalid. Similarly, D1.DATA is syntactically an input
signal, but it is only semantically valid when D1 is a DTYPE.

The DEVICES section now requires at least one device:

devices_section =
    "DEVICES" , "{" , device_decl , { device_decl } , "}" ;

This means an empty circuit is rejected by syntax rather than by a separate
semantic rule.

Device parameters have also been shortened. Instead of:

SW1 : SWITCH(initial = 0);
CLK1 : CLOCK(period = 4);
G1 : NAND(inputs = 2);

the revised language uses:

SW1 : SWITCH(0);
CLK1 : CLOCK(4);
G1 : NAND(2);

This is less wordy while remaining readable because the device type determines
the meaning of the parameter.


### Example Definition File

# Example: two cross-coupled NAND gates

DEVICES {
    SW1 : SWITCH(0);
    SW2 : SWITCH(1);

    G1 : NAND(2);
    G2 : NAND(2);
}

CONNECT {
    SW1 -> G1.I1;
    SW2 -> G2.I2;
    G1  -> G2.I1;
    G2  -> G1.I2;
}

MONITOR {
    G1, G2
};

END;


### DTYPE Example

DEVICES {
    CLK1 : CLOCK(4);
    DATA_SW : SWITCH(1);
    SET_SW : SWITCH(0);
    CLEAR_SW : SWITCH(0);
    D1 : DTYPE;
    G1 : AND(2);
}

CONNECT {
    DATA_SW  -> D1.DATA;
    CLK1     -> D1.CLK;
    SET_SW   -> D1.SET;
    CLEAR_SW -> D1.CLEAR;
    D1.Q     -> G1.I1;
    D1.QBAR  -> G1.I2;
}

MONITOR {
    CLK1, D1.Q, D1.QBAR, G1
};

END;


### Comments

The scanner should support two comment forms:

1. Open comments begin with # and continue until the end of the line.

# This whole line is a comment
G1 : NAND(2);  # This trailing comment is also ignored

2. Closed comments begin with /* and end with */. They may span multiple lines
and are useful for temporarily removing blocks of a definition file.

/*
DEVICES {
    UNUSED : SWITCH(0);
}
*/

Comments are removed by the scanner before symbols are passed to the parser, so
the comment syntax is not part of the EBNF grammar.


### Error Handling in Implementation

Syntax and semantic errors are handled at different stages.

Syntax errors are detected by the parser when the scanner's token sequence does
not match the EBNF. The parser does not need a separate list of all possible
syntax errors, because these are implicit in the grammar. A syntax error report
should be practical rather than theoretical: it should state the line, column,
unexpected token, and the construct expected at that point.

Example:

Line 4, column 14: syntax error: expected ';' after device declaration.

Semantic errors are detected after a construct has parsed successfully, when the
parser checks names, device types, ports and network connectivity against the
objects stored in the Devices, Network and Monitors modules.

Example:

Line 12, column 5: semantic error: input G1.I1 is connected more than once.


### Remaining Semantic Error Conditions

Duplicate device name
Detection: when adding a parsed device declaration, check whether its device
name already exists.
Report: semantic error: device name G1 is already defined.

Gate input count outside the allowed range
Detection: after parsing gate_kind(positive_integer), check that the number of
inputs is allowed by the Devices module.
Report: semantic error: gate G1 must have between 1 and 16 inputs.

Undefined device in connection
Detection: when checking a parsed connection, look up both device names in the
device table.
Report: semantic error: device G3 has not been declared.

Input port incompatible with declared device
Detection: compare the parsed input port with the declared type and properties
of the destination device.
Report: semantic error: G1.I5 is not a valid input for a 2-input NAND gate.

Output port incompatible with declared device
Detection: compare the parsed output form with the declared source device.
Single-output devices must be used without an output port, while DTYPE outputs
must be D1.Q or D1.QBAR.
Report: semantic error: DTYPE output must be specified as D1.Q or D1.QBAR.

Connection from non-output signal
Detection: resolve the left-hand side of a parsed connection and check that it
is a valid output of the declared device.
Report: semantic error: G1.I1 is an input and cannot be used as a connection
source.

Connection to non-input signal
Detection: resolve the right-hand side of a parsed connection and check that it
is a valid input of the declared device.
Report: semantic error: G1 is an output and cannot be used as a connection
destination.

Input connected more than once
Detection: store each connected input; report an error if another connection
targets the same input.
Report: semantic error: input G1.I1 is connected more than once.

Required input left unconnected
Detection: after all connections have been parsed, check every required input
of every device.
Report: semantic error: input G1.I2 is not connected.

Monitor placed on non-output signal
Detection: check each parsed monitor target against the output ports of its
declared device.
Report: semantic error: G1.I1 cannot be monitored because it is an input.

Duplicate monitor signal
Detection: store each monitor signal; report an error if a signal is listed
again.
Report: semantic error: monitor point G1 is listed more than once.
